  "query_preview": "..\n(Saturday through Wednesday)\nIssued at 228 PM CDT Thu Sep 24 2026\n\nUpper level flow becomes zonal Saturday as ridging builds back over \nour region. Low to medium (10-40%) rain chances become restricted to \nareas west of the Pecos River, mainly focused in/near the Davis \nMountains Saturday afternoon. Temperatures rebound into the 90s \nacross much of the region by Saturday afternoon, while 80s are \nforecast over SE NM and the higher terrain. Ridging continues to \nbuild Sunday, allowing for highs in the 90s to expand in coverage \nand further limiting rain chances near the Davis'. Another upper \ntrough is progged to begin approaching our area Monday, bringing \nbetter rain chances and a renewed concern for flash flooding \npotential, especially in our western zones. The trough itself looks \nto pass over our region Tuesday into Wednesday. As it stands, much \nof the region will have the potential to see isolated to scattered \nshowers/storms Monday afternoon, again mainly favoring our western \ncounties. Rain chances increase areawide Monday night and remain in \nthe forecast through Wednesday. Given saturated soils in parts of \nour area, flash flooding may be a hazard to monitor with this system \nduring events of heavy rainfall. Otherwise, lowering heights with \nthe trough, accompanied by an eventual cold front, send temperatures \non a cooling trend. Highs by Wednesday afternoon are forecast to \ngenerally reach the 80s. Keep posted for any updates going forward \nas this next system takes shape!",
